  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nStratiform rain continues to diminish as of midday, although there \nare still some areas of light rain ongoing south of US 24. Visible \nsatellite shows a few pockets of clearing as an MCV approaches the \nIL/IN border, but more widespread breaks in the clouds will be \nneeded to recharge the atmosphere for potential storms this \nafternoon and evening. It is worth noting that isolated strong to \nsevere storms are still possible despite the downgrade from SPC to a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe weather today. The \naforementioned MCV is expected to move across northern Indiana this \nafternoon and evening. Storm development is possible between 4-8 PM \nEDT today. Coverage will likely be spatially limited as the better \nenvironment is off to our southwest, however, I think the best \nchances for isolated severe weather today will be mainly east of I-\n69 and along/south of US 30. This would include Grant, Blackford, \nJay, Wabash, Adams, and Allen counties in Indiana and Defiance, \nHenry, Putnam, Paulding, Putnam, and Van Wert in Ohio. Models vary \nwidely on a solution, with some showing no storms and some showing \ndiscrete cells capable of producing severe weather. 30-40 kts of 0-\n6km shear, 100-200 m2/s2 of 0-1 SRH, and a narrow corridor of \nmoderate instability may develop along the I-69 corridor \ndepending on how much clearing we can get this afternoon. This \nwould be a favorable environment for damaging winds and an \nisolated, brief tornado. With a low end severe threat, this is a\nhighly conditional setup depending on clearing and how much the\natmosphere has been depleted by this morning's rain. Despite \nfavorable shear and instability, poor lapse low and mid level \nrates can be seen in forecast soundings from most of the CAMs, \nwhich will likely be a limiting factor.\n\nWPC does have a Moderate Risk (level 3 of 4) for excessive rainfall \nalong and south of US 30 today, where a Flood Watch is in effect. \nStrong moisture transport is expected and precipitable water values \nwill once again be at or above 2 inches today. With such high \nmoisture content in the airmass overhead (99th percentile PWATs and \ndewpoints well into the 70s), any storm could be a very efficient \nrainfall producer with rain rates between 1-2\" per hour. Given that \nthe ground is so saturated south of US 30, where 6-12\"+ of rain has \nfallen since this past Tuesday, renewed flash flooding in areas that \nhave already been hard hit is becoming an increasing concern. On\nthe very very low chance that an isolated tornado occurs in\neast-central Indiana somewhere in Wells, Adams, Grant, \nBlackford, and Jay counties where areal and river flooding is \nongoing, the best recommendation is to safely shelter in the \nlowest level away from windows and doors but to stay away from \nflooding. \n\nOne last round of scattered rain and storms is likely Sunday \nmorning, becoming more isolated into the afternoon hours. We will \nhave to monitor convection in eastern Iowa and northern Illinois \novernight that will move eastward. Unfortunately, the axis of \nheaviest rain looks to be south of the US 30 corridor before \ndaybreak Sunday as the LLJ ramps up with the stationary front in the \nvicinity. High PWATs and dewpoints will contribute to efficient,\nheavy rainfall late overnight into Sunday morning. The primary \ntimeframe for rain and storms looks to be between 4 AM to Noon \nEDT. An isolated strong storm is possible south of US 24, but \nonce again, flooding is expected to pose a higher threat \nspatially than severe weather. Due to this potential, the \ncurrent Flood Watch has been extended to last until 8 AM EDT \nSunday. \n\nA welcome break from daily rain and storm chances is ahead!  We \nfinally see a reprieve in the 'Ring of Fire' pattern Monday and \nTuesday as high pressure builds across the Great Lakes. This will \nallow for the area to dry out, floodwater to recede, and for \nriver levels to fall. Temperatures will be seasonable with highs\nin the upper 70s to near 80 degrees with lots of sun.\n\nAnother chance for rain and storms comes Wednesday with a cold \nfront, although it may be short lived as high pressure looks to \nreturn over the Great Lakes once again. After a very active first \nhalf of August and a Top 10 wettest August on record for some \nlocations already, we would happily welcome a stretch of dry weather \nby the end of the week into next weekend.",
